.PH "'hx Language'$Revision: 1.16 $'Specification'"
.PF "'Introduction' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.H 1 "Introduction"
.P
.B hx
is a domain-specific language for describing, computing, and
verifying cryptographic hash compositions.
It provides a precise, human-readable notation for expressing
the exact sequence of operations that produce a hash value
from an input password, salt, and optional additional parameters.
.P
The language was designed to satisfy three requirements that
emerged from over a decade of work with mdxfind, hashpipe,
and the broader password security research community:
.AL
.LI
.B "Reproducibility" \(em
every hash result that mdxfind reports must be independently
verifiable.
If mdxfind matches a truncated SHA-512 digest against a
32-character hex string, the hx expression
.ft CW
cut(sha512(pass), 0, 32)
.ft R
describes exactly what was computed.
Given the same password, evaluating that expression must produce
the identical 32-character string.
.LI
.B "Generality" \(em
the notation must be capable of expressing every hash type
that mdxfind supports (approximately 1000 types as of 2026),
as well as compositions that have not yet been assigned type
numbers.
A user encountering a novel hash scheme should be able to
describe it in hx without waiting for a new type to be added
to mdxfind.
.LI
.B "Clarity" \(em
the notation must be readable by humans who are not
cryptographers.
The expression
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
.ft R
should be immediately understandable as
\(lqcompute the MD5 of the password, concatenate the salt,
then compute the SHA-1 of the result.\(rq
.LE
.P
hx is not a general-purpose programming language.
It is deliberately constrained to operations relevant to
hash computation: cryptographic hash functions, string
concatenation, encoding transformations, iteration, and
simple control flow.
It is an interpreted language, and makes no attempt to
compete with mdxfind or hashcat in raw throughput.
Its value lies in correctness, expressiveness, and the
ability to serve as a canonical reference notation for
hash computations.
.H 2 "Scope and Boundaries"
.P
hx is designed to express
.I "compositions of cryptographic hash primitives"
\(em algorithms built by chaining, concatenating, iterating,
and encoding the outputs of hash functions.
This covers the vast majority of password hashing schemes
encountered in practice: simple hashes, salted hashes,
iterated hashes, compound multi-algorithm chains, and
HMAC (Hash-based Message Authentication Code) constructions.
.P
.B "Key derivation functions"
(KDFs) such as bcrypt, scrypt, yescrypt, Argon2, and PBKDF2
lie outside the expressive scope of hx's composition model.
These algorithms have internal state machines (Blowfish
key schedule expansion, memory-hard sequential access
patterns, configurable parallelism) that cannot be decomposed
into a sequence of hash-function calls.
They are registered in hx as
.I "opaque built-in functions"
\(em callable by name
.RB ( bcrypt() ,
.BR scrypt() ,
.BR pbkdf2_sha256() )
but not expressible as hx programs.
The appendix identifies these types and notes their
built-in status.
.P
This is a deliberate design boundary, not a gap.
A language that attempted to express bcrypt's internal
Blowfish rounds or scrypt's memory-access pattern would
require general-purpose byte manipulation, bitwise
operations, and array indexing, defeating the simplicity
and readability that are hx's primary design goals.
The practical consequence is small: KDFs are a closed set
of well-known algorithms, each with a standardized
specification and mature implementations.
Expressing their internals as hx programs would add
complexity without adding insight.
.P
hx operates on
.BR "raw bytes" .
The
.B pass
variable contains exactly the byte sequence provided
on the command line or read from standard input,
with no character encoding conversion and no Unicode
normalization applied.
When a hash scheme requires a specific encoding
(e.g., UTF-16LE for NTLM), the encoding must be
expressed explicitly in the hx program using a
transform function such as
.B utf16le() .
The language itself is encoding-agnostic:
a password is a sequence of bytes, and a hash function
receives a sequence of bytes.
.SK
.H 2 "Origin"
.P
The immediate impetus for hx was a problem encountered
during analysis of large hash collections with mdxfind.
When mdxfind searches a file of 32-character hex hashes,
it tests every supported algorithm that produces a hex
string of that length.
A match against
.B SHA512
is reported even though SHA-512 normally produces 128
hex characters \(em mdxfind matched only the first 32
characters, a prefix collision.
.P
The question that followed was deceptively simple:
\(lqhow do I verify this match?\(rq
No existing tool could take the description
\(lqfirst 32 hex characters of the SHA-512 of this password\(rq
and compute the result.
The user would need to write a small program, or chain
together command-line tools with careful attention to
encoding at each step.
.P
This is the problem hx solves.
The expression
.ft CW
cut(sha512(pass), 0, 32)
.ft R
is a complete, unambiguous specification that can be
evaluated by the hx interpreter to produce the exact
output that mdxfind matched.
From this starting point, the language grew to encompass
salted hashes, iterated hashes, compound hashes with
binary intermediates, and ultimately any composition of
cryptographic primitives that the password hashing world
has devised.
.H 2 "Relationship to Existing Tools"
.P
hx exists within the mdxfind tool ecosystem and is designed
to complement, not replace, the existing tools:
.BL
.LI
.B "mdxfind"
performs high-speed hash searching across hundreds of
algorithm types on CPU and GPU.
The hx language describes exactly what mdxfind computes,
in a form that can be independently verified.
An hx expression can be mapped to an mdxfind type number
(e.g.,
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
.ft R
corresponds to
.B "mdxfind \-m e587" ),
connecting the readable notation to the optimized engine.
.LI
.B "hashpipe"
is a hash verification tool.
When hx is integrated into hashpipe (via the
.B \-X
flag), all of hashpipe's 500+ hash function implementations
become available as hx primitives.
This is the recommended way to use hx for any work beyond
the basic hash types.
.LI
.B "mdsplit"
separates mdxfind output by type.
The type names that mdsplit produces (e.g., SHA1MD5PASSSALT)
correspond directly to hx expressions, providing a
translation path from mdxfind's compressed type notation
to the fully explicit hx form.
.LE
.H 2 "Modes of Use"
.P
hx operates in three modes of increasing complexity:
.AL
.LI
.B "Calculator mode."
A single expression on the command line, applied to one
password or to a stream of passwords on standard input.
This is the most common use: generating test vectors,
verifying individual hashes, or exploring a hash scheme.
.DS
.ft CW
hashpipe -X 'sha256(pass . salt)' -p password -s 12345
.ft R
.DE
.LI
.B "Script mode."
An hx program is read from a file, allowing multi-statement
computations with variables, loops, and conditionals.
This mode is required for hash schemes with complex
iteration patterns.
.DS
.ft CW
hashpipe -F phpbb3.hx -p password -s saltsalt
.ft R
.DE
.LI
.B "Batch mode."
In either calculator or script mode, passwords may be
read from standard input (one per line) instead of
being specified with
.BR \-p .
The hx program is compiled once and executed for each
input password.
.DS
.ft CW
cat wordlist.txt | hashpipe -X 'md5(pass . salt)' -s 12345
.ft R
.DE
.LE
.H 1 "Language Specification"
.PF "'Language Specification' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
This section defines the hx language: its lexical structure,
grammar, type system, evaluation rules, and built-in functions.
The specification is intended to be precise enough that an
independent implementation could produce identical output
for any well-formed hx program.
.H 2 "The hx Algebra"
.P
Every hx expression is built from five kinds of element:
.BL
.LI
.B "Variables"
\(em the read-only inputs
.RB ( pass ,
.BR salt ,
.BR salt2 ,
.BR pepper ,
.BR user )
and user-defined assignments.
.LI
.B "Literal strings"
\(em fixed byte sequences written in single or double
quotes.
Literals serve as HMAC keys, format prefixes
.RB ( "\(dq$H$9\(dq" ),
fixed delimiters, and any other byte sequence the
algorithm specifies in its definition.
.LI
.B "Integer literals"
\(em used as iteration counts, loop bounds, and
positional arguments to functions such as
.BR cut() ,
.BR bcrypt() ,
and the PBKDF2 family.
.LI
.B "Function calls"
\(em the computational primitives.
A function takes one or more string (and occasionally
integer) arguments and produces a single string result.
.LI
.B "The concatenation operator"
.BR . ,
which joins two byte sequences end to end.
Concatenation has the lowest expression precedence;
function calls bind more tightly than concatenation.
.LE
.P
.B "Function composition"
is the central operation.
The output of one function call may be supplied as the
input to another:
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass))
.ft R
computes MD5 of the password and then SHA-1 of that
result.
Composition is unrestricted: any function output may
feed any function input, and arbitrary nesting depth is
permitted.
The encoding contract (Section 2.5) determines whether
the inner output is delivered to the outer call as
canonical text (the default) or as raw bytes (when the
inner call carries a
.B _bin
suffix).
.P
.B "Output-role suffixes"
.RB ( _bin ,
.BR _hex ,
.BR _uc ,
.BR _b64 ,
.BR _mcf )
are attached to a function name to select a non-default
representation of its output.
The five suffixes form a small algebra of their own:
every function in the registry computes a canonical
result; the suffix controls how that result is encoded
on the way out.
A suffix that does not apply to a given function family
is a compile-time error.
.P
.B "The emit() function"
provides multi-value output.
A single hx program may
.B emit()
zero or more intermediate values during evaluation; the
program's final expression result is also emitted
unless an earlier
.B emit()
call suppresses the automatic emission.
This algebra is sufficient to describe hash schemes that
produce several output forms from a single password \(em
the
.B SHA1MD5HUM
family, the
.B PHPBB3
variants, the multi-form
.B SQL5
output, and any future scheme that follows the same
pattern.
.P
The remainder of this chapter formalises the syntax and
semantics of these constructs.
.H 2 "Lexical Structure"
.P
An hx program is a sequence of Unicode characters encoded
as UTF-8.
The lexer partitions the input into tokens of the following
classes:
.H 3 "Identifiers"
.P
An identifier begins with a letter (a\(enz, A\(enZ) or
underscore, followed by zero or more letters, digits, or
underscores:
.DS
.ft CW
[a\-zA\-Z_][a\-zA\-Z0\-9_]*
.ft R
.DE
.P
Identifiers serve as both variable names and function names.
The same identifier may refer to a variable or a function
depending on context: if followed by
.B (
or
.B ^
it is a function call; otherwise it is a variable reference.
.P
Hyphens are not permitted in identifiers.
Hash type names that contain hyphens (such as GOST-CRYPTO
or SHA3-256) use underscores in hx:
.ft CW
gost_crypto
.ft R
and
.ft CW
sha3_256\fR.
.ft R
Hashpipe automatically registers both the original hyphenated
name (for internal lookup) and the underscore variant (for
use in hx expressions).
.H 3 "Reserved Words"
.P
The following identifiers are reserved and may not be used
as variable names or function names:
.TS
box;
l l.
\fBWord\fP	\fBMeaning\fP
_
pass	current password
salt	primary salt value
salt2	secondary salt value
pepper	pepper value
user	user or userid value
for	loop statement
to	loop bound keyword
if	conditional statement
else	conditional alternative
.TE
.TB "Reserved Words"
.P
The variables
.BR pass ,
.BR salt ,
.BR salt2 ,
.BR pepper ,
and
.B user
are pre-populated from the command-line arguments
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-u ),
and are read-only; they cannot appear on the left side
of an assignment.
.H 3 "Integer Literals"
.P
An integer literal is a sequence of one or more digits,
optionally preceded by a minus sign:
.DS
.ft CW
42
1000
\-3
.ft R
.DE
.P
Integer values are used for iteration counts in the
.B ^
operator, loop bounds in
.B for
statements, and numeric arguments to functions such as
.BR cut() .
.H 3 "String Literals"
.P
A string literal is enclosed in double quotes or single quotes.
Standard backslash escapes are recognized within double-quoted
strings:
.DS
.ft CW
"hello world"
'$H$9'
"prefix\\tsuffix"
.ft R
.DE
.P
String literals produce byte sequences.
They are most commonly used for hash format prefixes
and fixed delimiters in output construction.
.H 3 "Operators and Delimiters"
.P
The following single and multi-character tokens are recognized:
.TS
box;
l l.
\fBToken\fP	\fBMeaning\fP
_
\&.	concatenation
^	iteration
 =	assignment
,	argument separator
( )	grouping and function call
{ }	block delimiters
==	equality comparison
!=	inequality comparison
< > <= >=	relational comparison
;	statement separator
.TE
.TB "Operators and Delimiters"
.H 3 "Statement Separators"
.P
Statements are separated by newlines or semicolons.
Both are equivalent and interchangeable.
Multiple consecutive separators (blank lines) are permitted
and ignored.
.P
Inside braces
.RB ( {
\&...
.BR } ),
newlines are treated as whitespace, not as statement
separators.
Within a block, statements must be separated by
semicolons:
.DS
.ft CW
for i = 1 to 10 {
    h = md5(h); k = sha1(k)
}
.ft R
.DE
.P
This rule has one practical consequence: the
.B else
keyword of an
.B if
statement must appear on the same line as the closing
brace of the then-block:
.DS
.ft CW
if h == target { x = 1 } else { x = 0 }
.ft R
.DE
.H 3 "Comments"
.P
A comment begins with
.B #
and extends to the end of the line.
Comments are ignored by the lexer:
.DS
.ft CW
# This is a comment
h = md5(pass)   # inline comment
.ft R
.DE
.SK
.H 2 "Grammar"
.P
The following grammar defines the syntax of hx programs
in extended BNF (Backus\(enNaur Form) notation.
Terminal symbols are shown in
.BR bold ;
non-terminals in
.I italic .
Optional elements are enclosed in brackets;
repetition is indicated by braces.
.TS
box;
l l.
\fIprogram\fP	\fIsep_opt stmt_list sep_opt\fP
\fIstmt_list\fP	\fIstmt\fP { \fIseps stmt\fP }
\fIstmt\fP	\fIassignment\fP | \fIfor_stmt\fP | \fIif_stmt\fP | \fIexpr\fP
\fIassignment\fP	\fBIDENT\fP \fB=\fP \fIexpr\fP
\fIfor_stmt\fP	\fBfor\fP \fBIDENT\fP \fB=\fP \fIexpr\fP \fBto\fP \fIexpr\fP \fIblock\fP
\fIif_stmt\fP	\fBif\fP \fIcondition\fP \fIblock\fP [ \fBelse\fP \fIblock\fP ]
\fIcondition\fP	\fIexpr\fP \fIrelop\fP \fIexpr\fP
\fIrelop\fP	\fB==\fP | \fB!=\fP | \fB<\fP | \fB>\fP | \fB<=\fP | \fB>=\fP
\fIblock\fP	\fB{\fP \fIsep_opt stmt_list sep_opt\fP \fB}\fP
\fIexpr\fP	\fIprimary\fP | \fIexpr\fP \fB.\fP \fIprimary\fP
\fIprimary\fP	\fBIDENT\fP | \fBreserved_var\fP | \fBNUMBER\fP
	| \fBSTRING\fP | \fIfuncall\fP | \fB(\fP \fIexpr\fP \fB)\fP
\fIfuncall\fP	\fBIDENT\fP \fB(\fP \fIarglist\fP \fB)\fP
	| \fBIDENT\fP \fB^\fP \fBNUMBER\fP \fB(\fP \fIarglist\fP \fB)\fP
\fIarglist\fP	\fIexpr\fP { \fB,\fP \fIexpr\fP }
\fIseps\fP	\fBSEP\fP { \fBSEP\fP }
\fIsep_opt\fP	[ \fIseps\fP ]
.TE
.FG "hx Grammar in Extended BNF"
.P
The concatenation operator
.B .
is left-associative and has the lowest precedence among
expression operators.
Function calls bind more tightly than concatenation.
.P
The grammar is implemented using GNU Bison (LALR(1) parser
generator) and Flex (lexical analyzer generator).
Three shift/reduce conflicts arise from the interaction of
optional leading separators with statement lists; all three
are resolved correctly by Bison's default shift preference.
.H 2 "Type System"
.P
hx has two value types:
.B strings
(byte sequences) and
.BR integers .
There is no Boolean type; conditions are evaluated by
comparison operators that consume two values and branch
accordingly.
.P
All hash function inputs and outputs are strings.
A string is an arbitrary sequence of bytes with an
associated length; it is not required to be valid UTF-8
or null-terminated.
Binary data (raw hash digests) and text data (hex-encoded
hashes, passwords, salts) are both represented as strings.
The language makes no distinction between them at the
type level; the distinction is maintained by convention
and by the
.B _bin
suffix on function names.
.P
Integers appear only in three contexts: iteration counts
in the
.B ^
operator, loop bounds and loop variables in
.B for
statements, and numeric arguments to functions such as
.BR cut() .
Integer values are 64-bit signed quantities.
.H 2 "The Encoding Contract"
.P
The most important design decision in hx is the default
encoding of hash function outputs.
This choice permeates the entire language and determines
whether compound hash expressions produce correct results.
.P
.B "The rule is simple:"
every hash function, HMAC, KDF, or crypt built-in
returns its
.I "canonical traditional form"
by default.
For fixed-length digests \(em
.BR md5 ,
.BR sha1 ,
.BR sha256 ,
.BR sha512 ,
.BR md4 ,
and the rest of the plain hash and HMAC families \(em
the canonical form is
.BR "lowercase hexadecimal" .
For variable-length key derivation functions \(em
.BR pbkdf2_* ,
.BR scrypt ,
.BR argon2id ,
.BR argon2i ,
.BR argon2d
\(em the canonical form is
.B "hex of the derived bytes"
(length = 2 \(mu dklen).
For structured-output crypt built-ins \(em
.BR bcrypt ,
.BR yescrypt ,
the
.B md5crypt
family,
.BR phpass
\(em the canonical form is the full
.BR "MCF (Modular Crypt Format) or PHC (Password Hashing Competition) string"
as it would appear in a shadow file
.RB ( $2b$12$... ,
.BR $y$j9T$... ,
.BR $1$salt$hash ,
.BR $H$9... ,
etc.).
.P
This rule was chosen because it matches the convention
used by the password hashing community.
When a security researcher writes
.ft CW
md5(md5($pass))
.ft R
they mean: compute the MD5 of the password, encode the
16-byte result as 32 lowercase hex characters, then
compute the MD5 of those 32 characters.
The outer MD5 receives a 32-byte ASCII input, not a
16-byte binary input.
This is what
.ft CW
md5(md5(pass))
.ft R
computes in hx.
By the same logic, a security researcher writing
.ft CW
bcrypt(pass, salt, 12)
.ft R
means the full 60-character
.B $2b$12$...
string \(em not the 31-character hash portion, and not
the raw 23-byte blowfish output.\*F
.FS
The internal blowfish state is 24 bytes wide, but the canonical
OpenBSD implementation encodes only 23 of those bytes into the
.B $2b$
hash portion to remain bug-compatible with the original
implementation.
The externally accessible raw form is therefore 23 bytes, not 24.
.FE
.H 3 "Output-role suffixes"
.P
To select a non-default representation of a function's
output, attach one of five
.I "output-role suffixes"
to the function name.
The suffix is stripped at compile time and sets a flag
on the
.B CALL
instruction; the base function always computes the same
underlying result, and the suffix controls only how that
result is returned.
.TS
box, tab(|);
l | l | lw(3.2i).
\fBSuffix\fP|\fBRole\fP|\fBMeaning\fP
_
(none)|canonical|Traditional form for this family.
_bin|binary|T{
Raw key material; digest bytes or derived bytes, before any encoding.
T}
_hex|hex|T{
Lowercase hex of \f(CW_bin\fP.
T}
_uc|uppercase hex|T{
Uppercase hex of \f(CW_bin\fP. Distinct from \fBupper()\fP under iteration: the role is what feeds the next round, so \f(CWmd5_uc^N\fP chains uppercase while \f(CWupper(md5^N)\fP uppercases only the final result. The two agree at N=1 and diverge from N=2 onward.
T}
_b64|base64|T{
Native base64 of the hash portion: bcrypt-base64 for \fBbcrypt\fP, crypt-base64 for the \fBmd5crypt\fP family, standard base64 elsewhere.
T}
_mcf|MCF/PHC|T{
Full crypt string. Defined only for families that have a standard MCF form; compile-time error otherwise.
T}
.TE
.TB "Output-role suffixes"
.P
For fixed-length digests, the default is identical to
.BR _hex ;
the invariant
.ft CW
md5(md5(pass)) == md5(md5_hex(pass))
.ft R
holds, and both are distinct from
.ft CW
md5(md5_bin(pass))
.ft R
\&.
For crypt built-ins the default is identical to
.BR _mcf ;
.ft CW
bcrypt(pass, salt, 12) == bcrypt_mcf(pass, salt, 12)
.ft R
\&.
.P
A worked example covers all five variants:
.TS
box;
l l.
\fBExpression\fP	\fBReturns\fP
_
\f(CWbcrypt(pass, salt, 12)\fR	60-char $2b$12$<salt22><hash31>
\f(CWbcrypt_mcf(pass, salt, 12)\fR	same as above (alias)
\f(CWbcrypt_b64(pass, salt, 12)\fR	31-char bcrypt-base64 hash portion
\f(CWbcrypt_bin(pass, salt, 12)\fR	23 raw bytes of blowfish output
\f(CWbcrypt_hex(pass, salt, 12)\fR	46-char hex of the 23 raw bytes
.TE
.TB "bcrypt output-role variants"
.P
Mixed chains work naturally.
.ft CW
sha1(bcrypt_bin(pass, salt, 12))
.ft R
feeds 23 raw bytes into SHA-1 and returns a 40-character
hex digest.
.ft CW
sha1(bcrypt(pass, salt, 12))
.ft R
feeds the full 60-character
.B $2b$12$...
string into SHA-1 \(em a different hash, and almost
certainly not what was intended.
.P
Encoding can also be made explicit using the
.B hex()
function.
The expression
.ft CW
hex(md5_bin(pass))
.ft R
is equivalent to
.ft CW
md5(pass)
.ft R
; it takes the raw binary MD5 output and encodes it as
hex.
This explicit form is useful when constructing output
formats or when clarity is more important than brevity.
.H 3 "Scope of the output-role suffixes"
.P
The five suffixes are defined for
.I "hash functions" ,
.I "HMAC functions" ,
.I "key derivation functions" ,
and
.I "crypt built-ins"
\(em any function whose output has a well-defined
canonical form that a caller might want to override.
Not every suffix is valid on every family.
In particular,
.B _mcf
is valid only on families that produce an MCF/PHC string
.RB ( bcrypt ,
.BR yescrypt ,
the
.B md5crypt
family,
.BR phpass ),
and
.B _b64
is valid only where a family has a well-defined
native-base64 hash portion.
Applying an unsupported suffix is a compile-time error,
the same rule that rejects
.B upper_bin
or
.BR base64_bin .
.P
Applying any output-role suffix to a string transform
function
.RB ( upper ,
.BR lower ,
.BR rev ,
.BR cut )
or a standalone encoding function
.RB ( base64 ,
.BR phpass_encode )
is a compile-time error.
These functions do not produce a canonical hash output
and have no encoding step to redirect.
.P
The
.B hex()
function is a special case.
.ft CW
hex_bin(x)
.ft R
is defined as the identity function: it returns its
input unchanged.
This is consistent with the semantics of
.B _bin
as \(lqskip the encoding step\(rq \(em
.B hex()
.I is
the encoding step, so skipping it returns the original input.
This identity is occasionally useful in generic expressions
but should not be relied upon for clarity.
.H 3 "Default output form by family"
.P
The following table summarizes the canonical return form
for each built-in family.
All entries in this table are implemented as hx
built-ins.
.TS H
expand allbox;
lw(1.6i) | lw(1.5i) | lw(3.0i).
\fBFamily\fP	\fBDefault return\fP	\fBExample\fP
_
.TH
.T&
lw(1.6i) | lw(1.5i) | lp8w(3.0i).
T{
Fixed-length digest
(md5, sha1, sha256,
sha512, md4, \&...)
T}	T{
lowercase hex
of digest
T}	T{
\fCsha1(pass)\fP = 40 hex chars
T}
_
T{
HMAC
(hmac_sha1, hmac_sha256,
hmac_sha512, \&...)
T}	T{
lowercase hex
T}	T{
\fChmac_sha256(k,m)\fP = 64 hex
T}
_
T{
PBKDF2
(pbkdf2_sha1, sha256,
sha512, md5)
T}	T{
hex of
derived bytes
T}	T{
\fCpbkdf2_sha256(p,s,1000,32)\fP = 64 hex
T}
_
scrypt	T{
hex of
derived bytes
T}	T{
\fCscrypt(p,s,N,r,p,dk)\fP = 2\(mudk hex
T}
_
T{
argon2
(argon2i, argon2d,
argon2id)
T}	T{
hex of
derived bytes
T}	T{
\fCargon2id(p,s,t,m,p,dk)\fP = 2\(mudk hex
T}
_
bcrypt	T{
$2b$...$ MCF
(60 chars)
T}	T{
\fCbcrypt(p,s,12)\fP = $2b$12$<22><31>
T}
_
yescrypt	T{
$y$...$ MCF
string
T}	T{
\fCyescrypt(p,s,...)\fP = $y$j9T$salt$hash
T}
_
T{
md5crypt
T}	$1$...$ MCF	\fCmd5crypt(p,s)\fP = $1$salt$hash
_
apr1	$apr1$...$ MCF	\fCapr1(p,s)\fP
_
sha256crypt	$5$...$ MCF	\fCsha256crypt(p,s,r)\fP
_
sha512crypt	$6$...$ MCF	\fCsha512crypt(p,s,r)\fP
_
