.TE
.TB "Default output form of each built-in family"
.H 2 "Evaluation Model"
.P
An hx program is processed in three phases:
parsing, compilation, and execution.
.H 3 "Parsing"
.P
The input text is tokenized by the Flex-generated lexer
and parsed by the Bison-generated parser into an abstract
syntax tree (AST).
Each node in the AST represents a language construct:
literal value, variable reference, function call,
concatenation, assignment, loop, conditional, or block.
.P
The parser handles the brace-depth-aware newline rule:
the lexer tracks the nesting depth of
.B {
\&...
.B }
blocks and emits newlines as separator tokens only at
depth zero.
.H 3 "Compilation"
.P
A single recursive walk of the AST produces a flat
array of bytecode instructions.
During this walk, the compiler:
.BL
.LI
Resolves variable names to integer slot indices.
The four built-in variables occupy fixed slots:
.B pass
= 0,
.B salt
= 1,
.B salt2
= 2,
.B pepper
= 3.
User-defined variables are assigned sequential slots
starting at 4.
.LI
Resolves function names to entries in the function registry.
If the name ends in
.BR _bin ,
the suffix is stripped and the
.I binary
flag is set on the
.B CALL
instruction.
If the base name is not found in the registry, compilation
fails with an error message identifying the unknown function
and the source line.
.LI
Collects string constants into a deduplication table.
.LI
Emits jump instructions for
.B for
loops and
.B if
conditionals, with forward references patched after
the target address is known.
.LI
Tracks stack depth to determine the maximum stack
size needed at runtime.
.LE
.P
The compiler produces a
.I program
structure containing the instruction array, the string
constant table, the variable name table (for diagnostics),
and the computed maximum stack depth.
.SK
.H 3 "Execution"
.P
The bytecode is executed by a stack-based virtual machine.
The VM maintains:
.BL
.LI
A
.I stack
of values (strings and integers), used for
expression evaluation.
.LI
A
.I variable array
indexed by slot number, pre-populated with the
built-in variables before each run.
.LI
A
.I "bump arena"
allocator for all per-password string data.
The arena is reset (rewound to offset zero) before
each password, ensuring that no memory is leaked
across runs and that allocation is a single pointer
increment.
.LE
.P
The VM executes instructions in a loop with a
.B switch
dispatch on the opcode.
The instruction set is:
.TS
box;
l l l.
\fBOpcode\fP	\fBOperand\fP	\fBEffect\fP
_
PUSH_VAR	slot	Push variable onto stack
PUSH_STR	index	Push string constant
PUSH_INT	value	Push integer
STORE	slot	Pop stack top into variable
CALL	entry, nargs, bin	Pop args, call function, push result
CONCAT	\(em	Pop two values, push concatenation
HALT	\(em	Stop; stack top is result
JUMP	address	Unconditional jump
INC	slot	Increment integer variable
JUMP_LE	address	Pop two integers, jump if a \(<= b
JUMP_EQ	address	Pop two values, jump if equal
JUMP_NE	address	Pop two values, jump if not equal
DUP	\(em	Duplicate stack top
POP	\(em	Discard stack top
.TE
.TB "Bytecode Instruction Set"
.P
The
.B CALL
instruction carries a direct pointer to the function
entry in the registry, resolved at compile time.
No name lookup occurs during execution.
The
.I binary
flag is also encoded in the instruction, so the
.B _bin
decision is made once at compile time, not per-password.
.P
The result of a program is the value on top of the stack
when the
.B HALT
instruction is reached.
In calculator mode, this value is written to standard output,
followed by a newline.
If the result is a binary string (from a
.B _bin
function), the raw bytes are written directly; the user
is responsible for applying
.B hex()
or another encoding function if text output is desired.
.H 2 "Diagnostics and Error Handling"
.P
hx defines the following error conditions.
A conforming implementation must report these errors
and must not silently produce incorrect output.
.H 3 "Compile-Time Errors"
.P
The following conditions are detected during compilation
and prevent execution:
.BL
.LI
.B "Unknown function" \(em
the identifier in a function call does not match any
entry in the function registry (after stripping
.BR _bin ,
if present).
The error message must identify the function name
and the source line.
.LI
.B "Invalid _bin application" \(em
the
.B _bin
suffix is applied to a function that is not a hash
function or HMAC function (see Section 2.4.1).
.LI
.B "Syntax error" \(em
the input does not conform to the grammar defined
in Section 2.2.
The error message should identify the source line
and the unexpected token.
.LE
.H 3 "Runtime Behavior"
.P
The following conditions may arise during execution.
hx defines their behavior to ensure reproducibility:
.BL
.LI
.B "Empty strings" \(em
an empty password, salt, or intermediate value is
a valid zero-length byte sequence.
Hash functions accept zero-length input and produce
their standard digest (e.g.,
.ft CW
md5("")
.ft R
produces
.ft CW
d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e\fR).
.ft R
.LI
.B "cut() bounds clamping" \(em
if the
.I start
argument exceeds the string length, the result is
an empty string.
If
.I start
+
.I length
exceeds the string length, the result is truncated
to the available bytes.
Negative
.I start
values that resolve to a position before the
beginning of the string are clamped to zero.
These are not errors.
.LI
.B "for loop with start > end" \(em
if the start value exceeds the end value, the loop
body is not executed.
This is not an error.
.LI
.B "Integer overflow" \(em
iteration counts and loop bounds are 64-bit signed
integers.
Values exceeding
.SU 2 63
\(en 1 produce
undefined results.
In practice, iteration counts above
.SU 2 32
are computationally infeasible and are likely
user errors.
.LI
.B "Stack overflow" \(em
the maximum stack depth is computed at compile time.
Programs that exceed implementation limits (deeply
nested function calls) may be rejected at compile
time or may produce undefined behavior at runtime.
.LI
.B "Binary data in text transforms" \(em
applying
.B upper()
or
.B lower()
to binary data (e.g., the output of a
.B _bin
function) operates on individual bytes using the
C locale.
Bytes that are not ASCII letters are passed through
unchanged.
This is defined behavior, not an error.
.LE
.H 2 "Program Structure"
.P
An hx program is a sequence of statements.
The last expression evaluated becomes the program's result.
.P
A single-expression program:
.DS
.ft CW
sha256(pass . salt)
.ft R
.DE
.P
A multi-statement program with the result on the last line:
.DS
.ft CW
h = md5_bin(salt . pass)
for i = 1 to 2047 {
    h = md5_bin(h . pass)
}
hex(h)
.ft R
.DE
.P
The final
.ft CW
hex(h)
.ft R
is an expression statement; its value is left on the stack
and becomes the program output.
.H 1 "Functions"
.PF "'Functions' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
Functions are the computational primitives of hx.
Every function takes one or more string arguments and
produces a string result.
Functions are registered in a flat table and looked up
by name at compile time.
.H 2 "Hash Functions"
.P
When hx is used via
.B "hashpipe \-X"
or
.BR "hashpipe \-F" ,
every hash type registered in hashpipe's type table
is available as an hx function.
The function name is the mdxfind type name, lowercased,
with hyphens replaced by underscores.
.P
The following table lists the hash functions corresponding
to mdxfind types e1 through e31 (the primitive hash
algorithms), plus selected higher-numbered types.
All functions accept the
.B _bin
suffix for raw binary output.
.TS
box;
l l l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBDigest bytes\fP	\fBHex chars\fP	\fBmdxfind type\fP
_
md5	16	32	e1 MD5
md4	16	32	e3 MD4
md2	16	32	e4 MD2
wrl	64	128	e5 WRL (Whirlpool)
hav128	16	32	e6 HAV128
sha0	20	40	e7 SHA0
sha1	20	40	e8 SHA1
sha224	28	56	e9 SHA224
sha256	32	64	e10 SHA256
sha384	48	96	e11 SHA384
sha512	64	128	e12 SHA512
gost	32	64	e13 GOST
gost_crypto	32	64	e14 GOST-CRYPTO
hav256	32	64	e15 HAV256
rmd128	16	32	e16 RMD128
rmd160	20	40	e17 RMD160
tiger	24	48	e18 TIGER
tth	24	48	e19 TTH
ed2k	16	32	e20 ED2K
aich	20	40	e21 AICH
has160	20	40	e22 HAS160
edon256	32	64	e23 EDON256
edon512	64	128	e24 EDON512
sne128	16	32	e25 SNE128
sne256	32	64	e26 SNE256
md6	16	32	e27 MD6
md6128	16	32	e28 MD6128
md6256	32	64	e29 MD6256
md6512	64	128	e30 MD6512
.TE
.TB "Primitive Hash Functions (e1\(ene30)"
.P
Types beyond e31 include additional Haval variants
(hav128_4, hav128_5, hav160_3, etc.), the full BLAKE,
BMW, CubeHash, ECHO, Fugue, Groestl, Hamsi, JH, Keccak,
SHA-3, Luffa, Shabal, SHAvite, SIMD, and Skein families,
as well as HMAC variants and keyed constructions.
All are available by their lowercased, underscore-normalized
names.
.P
.B "Uppercase hash types"
(such as MD5UC, e2) are not separate functions in hx.
The
.B upper()
transform function is used instead:
.ft CW
upper(md5(pass))
.ft R
is the hx equivalent of mdxfind type e2 (MD5UC).
This is more explicit: the uppercase encoding is a
formatting operation, not a property of the hash function.
.H 2 "HMAC Functions"
.P
HMAC functions take two arguments: the key and the message.
.DS
.ft CW
hmac_sha1(pass, salt)     # HMAC-SHA1, key=pass, msg=salt
hmac_sha256(pass, salt)   # HMAC-SHA256
.ft R
.DE
.P
The convention follows the standard HMAC definition:
the first argument is the key, the second is the message.
.H 2 "String Transform Functions"
.P
Transform functions operate on strings without performing
cryptographic hashing.
.TS
box;
l l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBArguments\fP	\fBDescription\fP
_
hex(x)	1	Encode bytes as lowercase hex
fromhex(x)	1	Decode hex string to bytes
upper(x)	1	Convert to uppercase
lower(x)	1	Convert to lowercase
rev(x)	1	Reverse byte sequence
rotate(x, N)	2	Rotate string by N positions
cap(x)	1	Capitalize first lowercase letter
cap(x, N)	2	Capitalize letter at position N
rot13(x)	1	ROT13 alphabetic rotation
pad(x, N)	2	Null-pad to N bytes
xor(a, b)	2	Byte-wise exclusive OR
and(a, b)	2	Byte-wise AND
or(a, b)	2	Byte-wise OR
wperm(x, w0, w1, ...)	2+	Permute 4-byte words
bswap32(x)	1	Reverse bytes within each 4-byte group
length(x)	1	Byte length (returns integer)
.TE
.TB "String Transform Functions"
.P
The
.B hex()
function is particularly important for explicit encoding
control.
The expression
.ft CW
hex(md5_bin(pass))
.ft R
is equivalent to
.ft CW
md5(pass)\fR;
.ft R
both produce the same 32-character hex string.
The explicit form is preferred when the intent is to
emphasize that a binary intermediate is being encoded
for output.
.P
The
.B rotate()
function rotates a string by N character positions.
Positive N rotates right (last N characters move to
the front); negative N rotates left (first |N|
characters move to the end):
.DS
.ft CW
rotate("abcdef", 2)    # "efabcd"
rotate("abcdef", -2)   # "cdefab"
rotate(md5(pass), 5)   # MD5 hex rotated right by 5
.ft R
.DE
.P
This function is essential for expressing rotated hash
matches, where the hex representation of a hash has been
cyclically shifted before storage.
mdxfind detects these as
.ft CW
rotNN_TYPExMM\fR
.ft R
in its output.
The hx equivalent is
.ft CW
rotate(type(pass), N)\fR.
.ft R
.P
The
.B xor()
function performs byte-wise exclusive OR on two byte
sequences.
If the arguments are of unequal length, the shorter
argument is cycled (repeated) to match the length of
the longer.
This is primarily useful for expressing the internal
structure of HMAC (ipad/opad XOR) and for obfuscation
schemes that XOR data with a key:
.DS
.ft CW
xor(sha1_bin(pass), salt)
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B and()
and
.B or()
functions perform byte-wise AND and OR respectively,
with the same cycling semantics as
.BR xor() .
These are useful for masking digest bytes:
.DS
.ft CW
hex(and(sha1_bin(pass), "\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\xff..."))
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B cap()
function capitalizes a letter within a string.
With one argument, it finds and capitalizes the first
lowercase ASCII letter.
With two arguments, it capitalizes the letter at the
specified position:
.DS
.ft CW
cap(md5(pass))         # first lowercase letter
cap(md5(pass), 0)      # character at position 0
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B rot13()
function applies ROT13 alphabetic rotation to each
ASCII letter, leaving non-letters unchanged.
.P
The
.B pad()
function null-pads a value to a fixed length.
If the input is shorter than the specified length,
zero bytes are appended; if longer, it is truncated.
This is required for schemes that use fixed-width fields:
.DS
.ft CW
md5(pad(pass, 100))    # RADMIN2
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B bswap32()
function reverses the byte order within each 4-byte
group of its argument, performing a 32-bit
byte-swap on every complete group.
If the input length is not a multiple of 4, the
trailing 1\(en3 bytes are copied unchanged:
.BL
.LI
.ft CW
bswap32(fromhex("aabbccdd"))
.ft R
\(-> 0xddccbbaa (4 bytes: one full group reversed)
.LI
.ft CW
bswap32(fromhex("aabbccddee"))
.ft R
\(-> 0xddccbbaa 0xee (5 bytes: one group reversed, one byte unchanged)
.LI
.ft CW
bswap32(fromhex("aabb"))
.ft R
\(-> 0xaabb (2 bytes: no full group, copied as-is)
.LI
.ft CW
bswap32("")
.ft R
\(-> "" (empty)
.LE
.P
The function is intended for converting between
big-endian and little-endian representations of 32-bit
word arrays, as commonly encountered in hash digest
output.
Applied to text, the result is meaningless.
.P
The
.B wperm()
function permutes 4-byte words within a byte sequence.
The first argument is the data; subsequent arguments are
zero-based word indices specifying the new order:
.DS
.ft CW
hex(wperm(md5_bin(pass), 3, 2, 0, 1))  # words d,c,a,b
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B length()
function returns the byte length of its argument as an
integer.
The result has both an integer value (for use in loop
bounds and comparisons) and a string representation
(for concatenation):
.DS
.ft CW
for i = 0 to sub(length(pass), 1) {
    h = h . md5(cut(pass, i, 1))
}
.ft R
.DE
.H 2 "Extraction Functions"
.P
Extraction functions select portions of a string.
.H 3 "cut()"
.P
The
.B cut()
function extracts a substring, with semantics similar to
the C library function
.BR substr() .
It accepts one, two, or three arguments:
.TS
box;
l l.
\fBForm\fP	\fBMeaning\fP
_
cut(x)	identity (returns x unchanged)
cut(x, start)	from start to end of string
cut(x, start, length)	length characters from start
.TE
.TB "cut() Function Forms"
.P
The
.I start
position is zero-based.
A negative
.I start
counts from the end of the string:
.DS
.ft CW
cut("abcdef", 2, 3)     # "cde"
cut("abcdef", \-3)        # "def"
cut("abcdef", \-3, 2)     # "de"
.ft R
.DE
.H 3 "trunc()"
.P
.B trunc()
is a convenience alias for
.ft CW
cut(x, 0, n)\fR:
.ft R
.DS
.ft CW
trunc(sha512(pass), 32)  # first 32 hex characters
.ft R
.DE
.H 2 "Encoding Functions"
.H 3 "base64()"
.P
Standard Base64 encoding as defined in RFC 4648, with
.B =
padding characters.
Primarily used with
.B _bin
outputs to produce base64-encoded hash digests:
.DS
.ft CW
base64(md5_bin(pass))
.ft R
.DE
.H 3 "phpass_encode()"
.P
The itoa64 encoding used by phpass, PHPBB3, and WordPress.
This encoding uses the 64-character alphabet
.ft CW
\&./0-9A-Za-z
.ft R
with little-endian 6-bit packing.
A 16-byte MD5 digest produces 22 encoded characters.
.DS
.ft CW
phpass_encode(md5_bin(pass))
.ft R
.DE
.P
This function is essential for producing the correct
output format for PHPBB3 and WordPress hashes, where
the final hash value is not hex-encoded but uses this
custom alphabet.
.H 3 "sha512crypt_encode()"
.P
The custom base64 encoding used by SHA-512 crypt
.RB ( $6$ ).
Uses the same alphabet as phpass
.RB ( ./0-9A-Za-z )
but with a different byte permutation and big-endian
bit packing specific to the crypt(3) specification.
.H 3 "bcrypt_encode()"
.P
The custom base64 encoding used by bcrypt, with alphabet
.ft CW
\&./ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789\fR.
.ft R
.H 3 "frombase64()"
.P
Decodes a standard base64 string (RFC 4648) to raw bytes.
The inverse of
.BR base64() .
.BR = -padding
is handled correctly.
This is required for hash types where the salt is
stored in base64 encoding:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(utf16le(pass) . frombase64(salt))
.ft R
.DE
.H 3 "fromhex()"
.P
Decodes a hexadecimal string to raw bytes.
The inverse of
.BR hex() .
Each pair of hex characters produces one byte.
.H 3 "progress_encode()"
.P
The custom base64-style encoding used by Progress
OpenEdge passwords.
Operates on a 16-byte MD5 digest and produces a
22-character output drawn from the alphabet
.ft CW
\&./0-9A-Za-z\fR.
.ft R
The byte-permutation and bit-packing differ from
both
.B phpass_encode()
and
.BR sha512crypt_encode() ;
the function exists to faithfully reproduce the
on-disk Progress OpenEdge format.
.H 3 "juniper_encode()"
.P
The Type-9 / "$9$" password encoding used by Juniper
Junos OS.
Operates on a 16-byte MD5 digest and produces a
13-character ASCII output using a fixed permuted
alphabet defined by Juniper.
The result is the
.B $9$
secret stored in
.B configuration
files.
.H 3 "cisco_pix_encode()"
.P
The 16-character Base64 encoding used by Cisco PIX
and ASA enable/local password hashes.
Operates on a 16-byte MD5 digest and produces a
16-character output using the alphabet
.ft CW
\&./0-9A-Za-z\fR
.ft R
with PIX/ASA-specific bit packing.
The PIX password algorithm itself is
.IB md5 (pad(pass\ .\ salt,\ 16))
followed by this encoding;
.B cisco_pix_encode()
covers only the encoding step.
.H 3 "lotus64_encode()"
.P
The Lotus / Domino base64-style encoding used in the
Domino-6 password format.
Operates on a 10-byte buffer (8 hash bytes plus 2
length/header bytes) and produces a 14-character
output, which is the body of an
.B (Gxxxxxxxxxxxxx)
Domino-6 hash.
.H 3 "besder_encode()"
.P
The 8-character lossy fold used by Besder camera
authentication (mdxfind type e963).
Accepts a 16-byte input (typically an MD5 digest);
for each of the four 4-byte groups, two characters
are emitted from the alphabet
.ft CW
0-9A-Za-z\fR
.ft R
using the rule
.RB ( ( b 0\fB+\fPb 1 )
.B & 0xff
.B "\\(mu 62" ).
The expression
.ft CW
besder_encode(md5_bin(pass))
.ft R
reproduces the BESDER-AUTH (e960) hash format.
.H 3 "dahua_encode()"
.P
The Dahua-camera variant of the same fold used by
.BR besder_encode() ,
without the
.B & 0xff
byte truncation
.RB ( ( b 0\fB+\fPb 1 )
.BR "\\(mu 62" ).
This corresponds to mdxfind type e959 (DAHUA-AUTH).
.H 2 "Cipher Functions"
.P
Cipher functions wrap the OpenSSL block-cipher
primitives used by hash schemes that include an
explicit encrypt/decrypt step.
All produce raw ciphertext or plaintext bytes;
the default rendering is lowercase hex, but the
.BR _bin ,
.BR _hex ,
and
.B _b64
suffixes apply per the encoding contract.
.TS
box;
l l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBArguments\fP	\fBDescription\fP
_
des(key, pt)	2	Single-DES ECB encrypt of one 8-byte block
des3(key, pt)	2	Triple-DES (EDE) ECB encrypt of one 8-byte block
aes_ecb_encrypt(key, pt)	2	AES-ECB encrypt; key 16/24/32 bytes
aes_cbc_encrypt(key, iv, pt)	3	AES-CBC encrypt, no padding
aes_cbc_decrypt(key, iv, ct)	3	AES-CBC decrypt, no padding strip
aes_unwrap(kek, wrapped)	2	RFC 3394 AES Key Unwrap
.TE
.TB "Cipher Functions"
.P
.B des()
and
.B des3()
operate on a single 8-byte block.
The key is zero-padded (or truncated) to 8 or 24
bytes; the plaintext is padded to 8 bytes if shorter.
These cover legacy schemes such as Oracle 7
.RB ( oracle7() ,
which calls
.BR des() ),
AS/400 password hashes
.RB ( as400_des() ),
and Domino-5 challenge mixing.
.P
.B aes_ecb_encrypt()
encrypts a positive multiple of 16 bytes under
AES-128, AES-192, or AES-256, selected by the key
length.
This is the building block for the AES-NOKDF family
(e963/e964/e965), where the password is zero-padded
to the key size and the salt is the single-block
plaintext:
.DS
.ft CW
aes_ecb_encrypt(pad(pass, 32), fromhex(salt))
.ft R
.DE
.P
.B aes_cbc_encrypt()
and
.B aes_cbc_decrypt()
operate on positive multiples of 16 bytes with a
16-byte IV.
Neither function performs PKCS#7 padding or
strip-on-output: the caller is responsible for
preparing or verifying the padding bytes
explicitly.
This deliberate choice exposes the raw block
behavior so that schemes which use a non-standard
padding marker (e.g.\ Veeam VBK's 12-byte
.B 0x0c
trailer) can verify the marker themselves with a
.B cut()
plus equality test:
.DS
.ft CW
dk = pbkdf2_sha1_bin(utf16le(pass), salt, iter, 48)
pt = aes_cbc_decrypt_bin(cut(dk,0,32), cut(dk,32,16), ct)
if cut(pt,4,12) == fromhex("0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c") { emit("ok") }
.ft R
.DE
.P
.B aes_unwrap()
implements the RFC 3394 AES Key Wrap reverse
operation.
The key-encryption key
.RI ( kek )
must be 16 or 32 bytes (AES-128 or AES-256), and the
wrapped input must be
.IR 8\ +\ 8 n
bytes for some
.I "n >= 1" .
On a successful unwrap (the leading IV matches
.BR 0xa6a6a6a6a6a6a6a6 ),
the function returns the
.IR 8 n -byte
plaintext;
on IV mismatch, it returns the empty string.
The empty/non-empty distinction is the standard way
to verify Apple FileVault/APFS passwords:
.DS
.ft CW
length(aes_unwrap(pbkdf2_sha256_bin(pass, salt, iter, 32), wkey)) > 0
.ft R
.DE
.H 2 "Proprietary Algorithm Primitives"
.P
A small number of hash schemes use proprietary,
hand-built password mixers that are not derived from
standard hash or KDF building blocks.
For each such scheme the algorithm has been ported
verbatim from
.B mdxfind.c
and exposed as an opaque hx primitive.
.TS
box;
l l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBArguments\fP	\fBDescription\fP
_
domino5(pass)	1	Lotus Domino 5 challenge mixer
domino6(pass)	1	Lotus Domino 6 password hash (calls lotus64_encode)
oracle7(user, pass)	2	Oracle 7 / 8 / 9 / 10 user-and-password DES chain
sap_bcode(user, pass)	2	SAP CODVN B (BCODE) password digest
as400_des(user, pass)	2	IBM AS/400 OS/400 DES-based password hash
axcrypt(pass)	1	AxCrypt key-file mixing function
racf_encrypt(user, pass)	2	IBM RACF DES-based password hash
racf_kdfaes(pass, user, hdr, salt)	4	IBM RACF KDFAES (HMAC-SHA256 memory mixer)
.TE
.TB "Proprietary Algorithm Primitives"
.P
Each of these primitives is documented in a
trailing footnote of
.BR hx (8)
.RI "(Appendix A,"
.IR "Notes [1]\(en[32]" ),
which gives the C reference implementation, the
exact buffer layout, and any required input
preprocessing.
.P
The default rendering for the digest-producing
primitives
.RB ( oracle7() ,
.BR sap_bcode() ,
.BR as400_des() ,
.BR axcrypt() ,
.BR racf_encrypt() ,
.BR racf_kdfaes() ,
.BR domino5() )
is lowercase hex; the
.BR _bin ,
.BR _hex ,
.B _b64
suffixes apply.
The encoder-style primitives
.RB ( domino6() ,
.BR lotus64_encode() ,
.BR juniper_encode() ,
.BR cisco_pix_encode() ,
.BR progress_encode() ,
.BR besder_encode() ,
.BR dahua_encode() )
return ASCII text and accept no role suffix.
.H 2 "Character Encoding Functions"
.P
Character encoding functions convert the byte
representation of a string.
.TS
box;
l l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBArguments\fP	\fBDescription\fP
_
utf16le(x)	1	Convert UTF-8 to UTF-16 little-endian
utf16be(x)	1	Convert UTF-8 to UTF-16 big-endian
utf7(x)	1	Convert to UTF-7 encoding
zext16(x)	1	Zero-extend each byte to 16 bits little-endian
from_cp1252(x)	1	Convert UTF-8 to Windows-1252 (Latin-1 superset)
from_cp1251(x)	1	Convert UTF-8 to Windows-1251 (Cyrillic)
ebcdic(x)	1	Convert ASCII to IBM EBCDIC (CP037)
.TE
.TB "Character Encoding Functions"
.P
.B utf16le()
is essential for NTLM and related Microsoft hash types,
which hash the UTF-16LE encoding of the password rather
than its UTF-8 or ASCII representation:
.DS
.ft CW
md4(utf16le(pass))     # NTLM
.ft R
.DE
.P
The conversion handles full Unicode: multi-byte UTF-8
sequences are properly decoded and re-encoded as
UTF-16, including surrogate pairs for characters above
U+FFFF.
.P
.B zext16()
performs the same byte-level expansion as
.B utf16le()
but without UTF-8 decoding: every input byte is paired
with a trailing 0x00, regardless of whether the input
is valid UTF-8.
This is used by hash schemes that name themselves after
UTF-16LE but in practice operate on the raw byte stream.
.P
.B from_cp1252()
and
.B from_cp1251()
convert a UTF-8 string to the corresponding single-byte
Windows code page.
Characters that are not representable in the target code
page are passed through as
.B '?' .
These conversions are required by hashes which were
historically computed over the legacy single-byte
encoding rather than UTF-8.
.P
.B ebcdic()
converts an ASCII string to IBM EBCDIC code page 037
(the standard EBCDIC mapping used in z/OS RACF and
related mainframe contexts).
The companion EBCDIC variant CP1047 is used internally
by
.B racf_kdfaes()
and is not exposed as a separate primitive.
.H 2 "Arithmetic Functions"
.P
Arithmetic functions operate on integer values and
return integer results (with a string representation
for use in string context).
.TS
box;
l l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBArguments\fP	\fBDescription\fP
_
add(a, b)	2	Integer addition
sub(a, b)	2	Integer subtraction
mul(a, b)	2	Integer multiplication
mod(a, b)	2	Integer modulo (b = 0 returns 0)
length(x)	1	Byte length of x
.TE
.TB "Arithmetic Functions"
.P
These functions enable computed loop bounds and
conditional iteration patterns:
.DS
.ft CW
for i = 0 to sub(length(pass), 1) {
    h = h . md5(cut(pass, i, 1))
}
.ft R
.DE
.H 2 "Output Function"
.H 3 "emit()"
.P
The
.B emit()
function writes its argument to standard output,
followed by a newline, and returns the value unchanged
(passthrough).
It is used for hash types that produce multiple
output values from a single password:
.DS
.ft CW
h = md5(pass)
emit(md5(pass . h))
emit(md5(pass . ":" . h))
.ft R
.DE
.P
When a program contains any
.B emit()
calls, the automatic final-value output is suppressed.
Without
.BR emit() ,
the last expression value is output as before.
This rule is unambiguous: the presence of
.B emit()
in the compiled program determines the output mode.
.P
If
.B emit()
receives binary data (any byte outside the printable
ASCII range 0x20\(en0x7e), it automatically hex-encodes
the output.
This prevents raw binary from corrupting the output
stream when
.B _bin
results are emitted.
.SK
.H 2 "Key Derivation Functions"
.P
KDF functions are opaque built-ins that call native
implementations.
They accept integer parameters for cost/iteration
factors:
.TS
box;
l l.
\fBFunction\fP	\fBSignature\fP
_
bcrypt	bcrypt(pass, salt, cost)
scrypt	scrypt(pass, salt, N, r, p, dklen)
argon2id	argon2id(pass, salt, time, mem_kb, parallel, dklen)
argon2i	argon2i(pass, salt, time, mem_kb, parallel, dklen)
argon2d	argon2d(pass, salt, time, mem_kb, parallel, dklen)
yescrypt	yescrypt(pass, setting)
pbkdf2_sha1	pbkdf2_sha1(pass, salt, iterations, dklen)
pbkdf2_sha256	pbkdf2_sha256(pass, salt, iterations, dklen)
pbkdf2_sha512	pbkdf2_sha512(pass, salt, iterations, dklen)
pbkdf2_md5	pbkdf2_md5(pass, salt, iterations, dklen)
.TE
.TB "Key Derivation Functions"
.P
PBKDF2 functions are available in both standalone and
hashpipe builds (via OpenSSL).
The remaining KDFs require hashpipe integration.
.P
.B bcrypt()
returns the full crypt string
.ft CW
($2b$CC$salt+hash)\fR
.ft R
by default, or the hash portion only with
.BR _bin .
The cost parameter ranges from 4 to 31 (default 12).
.P
.B scrypt()
parameters: N = CPU/memory cost (power of 2, default 16384),
r = block size (default 8), p = parallelism (default 1),
dklen = output length (default 32).
.P
.B argon2id()
parameters: time = iterations (default 3),
mem_kb = memory in KB (default 65536),
parallel = lanes (default 4),
dklen = output length (default 32).
.H 1 "Iteration and Control Flow"
.PF "'Iteration and Control Flow' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
Many password hashing schemes apply a hash function
repeatedly to increase computational cost, or branch
on a comparison of a derived value against a known
target.
hx provides three mechanisms for expressing iteration
and conditional execution: the
.B ^
operator, the
.B for
loop, and the
.B if
statement.
.H 2 "The ^ Operator"
.P
The caret operator provides concise notation for simple
re-hashing, where the output of one round becomes the
sole input to the next:
.DS
.ft CW
md5^1000(pass)
.ft R
.DE
.P
This expression applies
.B md5()
to the password, then applies
.B md5()
to the result, 999 more times, for a total of 1000
applications.
The encoding rule applies at each step: each round
receives the hex output of the previous round.
.P
With the
.B _bin
suffix, the raw bytes are chained instead:
.DS
.ft CW
md5_bin^1000(pass)
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B ^
operator is syntactic sugar.
The expression
.ft CW
md5^3(pass)
.ft R
is exactly equivalent to:
.DS
.ft CW
md5(md5(md5(pass)))
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B ^
operator is limited to the case where the function is
applied to its own output with no additional inputs mixed
in per round.
For schemes that mix salt, password, or other data at each
iteration, the
.B for
loop is required.
.H 2 "The for Loop"
.P
The
.B for
statement provides explicit iteration with a named loop
variable, a start value, and an end value (inclusive):
.DS
.ft CW
for \fIvar\fP = \fIstart\fP to \fIend\fP {
    \fIbody\fP
}
.ft R
.DE
.P
The loop variable is an integer that is incremented by
one after each iteration of the body.
The body is executed while the variable is less than or
equal to the end value.
.P
The
.B for
loop is required for hash schemes where additional data
is mixed into each iteration.
The canonical example is PHPBB3 (phpass), which
concatenates the previous hash result with the original
password at each round:
.DS
.ft CW
h = md5_bin(salt . pass)
for i = 1 to 2047 {
    h = md5_bin(h . pass)
}
hex(h)
.ft R
.DE
.P
This cannot be expressed with the
.B ^
operator because the loop body is not simply
\(lqapply the function to its own output\(rq \(em
it concatenates
.B pass
at each step.
.P
The initial assignment
.ft CW
h = md5_bin(salt . pass)
.ft R
is the first of 2048 total MD5 operations.
The loop runs 2047 more iterations, each taking the
binary output of the previous round, concatenating the
password, and computing a new MD5.
The final
.ft CW
hex(h)
.ft R
converts the last binary digest to hex for output.
.H 2 "The if Statement"
.P
The
.B if
statement executes a block conditionally based on the
result of a relational expression.
.DS
.ft CW
if \fIexpr\fP \fIrelop\fP \fIexpr\fP {
    \fIthen-body\fP
} else {
    \fIelse-body\fP
}
.ft R
.DE
.P
The
.B else
clause is optional.
The condition is a single relational test, not a chain
of Boolean expressions; there are no
.B and ,
.B or ,
or
.B not
operators on Booleans because hx has no Boolean type.
The supported relops are
.BR == ,
.BR != ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR <= ,
.BR >= .
.P
Comparison semantics are strict-typed:
integer\(emversus\(eminteger compares signed magnitudes,
string\(emversus\(emstring compares with
.BR memcmp ,
and a mixed integer/string comparison raises a runtime
error rather than coercing.
A comparison such as
.ft CW
length(pass) > 8
.ft R
works because both sides are integers;
a comparison such as
.ft CW
cut(d, 4, 12) == fromhex("0c0c0c0c0c...")
.ft R
works because both sides are strings.
.P
The intended use of
.B if
is to drive
.B emit()
from a positive verification, so that the program
prints something on a match and nothing otherwise:
.DS
.ft CW
dk = pbkdf2_sha1_bin(utf16le(pass), fromhex(salt), iter, 48)
pt = aes_cbc_decrypt_bin(cut(dk,0,32), cut(dk,32,16), fromhex(ct))
if cut(pt,4,12) == fromhex("0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c") { emit("ok") }
.ft R
.DE
.P
This pattern fits hash schemes whose verification step
is a structural property of a decrypted value (Veeam
VBK, Apple FileVault/APFS, certain WPA flavors)
rather than a hash equality.
The grammar requires the closing brace and the
.B else
keyword to appear on the same line; see
.B "Statement Separators"
in the Lexical Structure chapter.
.H 1 "Compound Hash Expressions"
.PF "'Compound Hash Expressions' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
The power of hx lies in its ability to express arbitrary
compositions of hash functions.
A compound hash applies multiple functions in sequence,
with the output of one becoming the input to the next,
optionally concatenated with salt or other data.
.H 2 "Simple Composition"
.P
Functions nest naturally:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass))
.ft R
.DE
.P
This computes MD5(password) as a 32-character hex string,
then computes SHA-1 of those 32 characters.
The result is a 40-character hex string.
It corresponds to mdxfind type
.BR SHA1MD5PASS .
.H 2 "Composition with Salt"
.P
Salt is introduced via concatenation:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
.ft R
.DE
.P
This computes MD5(password) as hex, concatenates the salt,
then computes SHA-1 of the combined string.
It corresponds to mdxfind type
.B SHA1MD5SALT
(e587).
.P
The placement of salt relative to other components is
significant.
The following are all different computations:
.DS
.ft CW
md5(pass . salt)       # password then salt
md5(salt . pass)       # salt then password
md5(salt . pass . salt)  # salt, password, salt
.ft R
.DE
.P
These correspond to mdxfind types
.BR MD5PASSSALT ,
.BR MD5SALTPASS ,
and
.B MD5SALTPASSSALT
respectively.
.H 2 "Binary Intermediates"
.P
When two hash functions are chained with a binary
intermediate (the raw digest bytes rather than their
hex encoding), the inner function uses the
.B _bin
suffix:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(md5_bin(pass))     # feed 16 raw bytes to SHA-1
sha1(md5(pass))         # feed 32 hex chars to SHA-1
.ft R
.DE
.P
The first expression corresponds to mdxfind type
.B SHA1MD5RAW
(e641); the second to
.B SHA1MD5PASS
(e161).
These produce different results and must not be confused.
.P
Binary intermediates are common in real-world hash schemes.
MySQL's native password format uses:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(sha1_bin(pass))
.ft R
.DE
.P
Many LDAP SSHA schemes concatenate the raw hash with the
salt before base64-encoding the result.
The
.B _bin
suffix makes these compositions explicit and unambiguous.
.H 2 "Constructing Output Formats"
.P
Some hash schemes have distinctive output formats that
include type identifiers, salt values, and the encoded
hash in a specific arrangement.
hx expressions can construct these formats using string
literal concatenation.
.P
PHPBB3 output format:
.DS
.ft CW
"$H$9" . salt . phpass_encode(h)
.ft R
.DE
.P
A hypothetical format with a prefix and base64:
.DS
.ft CW
"{SSHA}" . base64(sha1_bin(pass . salt) . salt)
.ft R
.DE
.P
These expressions produce the complete output string,
including all formatting, ready for comparison against
a hash file.
.H 1 "Hashpipe Integration"
.PF "'Hashpipe Integration' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
hx is fully integrated into hashpipe. It has access
to the full set of hash functions that hashpipe supports.
.H 2 "Function Registration"
.P
At startup, when the
.B \-X
or
.B \-F
flag is present, hashpipe initializes its type registry
(the same initialization used for normal hash verification)
and then walks the registry to register each type as an
hx function.
.P
For each hashpipe type that has a
.I compute
function and a positive hash length, a bridge entry is
created in the hx function registry.
The bridge entry stores a pointer to the hashpipe compute
function and the digest size in bytes.
When the hx VM executes a
.B CALL
instruction for a bridged function, it invokes the generic
bridge wrapper, which:
.AL
.LI
Extracts the input data from the hx value on the stack.
.LI
Calls the hashpipe compute function with the standard
signature
.ft CW
(pass, passlen, salt, saltlen, dest).
.ft R
.LI
Hex-encodes the digest (or returns raw binary if the
.B _bin
flag is set).
.LI
Stores the result in the arena and pushes it onto the
hx stack.
.LE
.P
This bridge mechanism means that adding a new hash function
to hashpipe automatically makes it available in hx
expressions, with no changes to the hx source code.
.H 2 "Command-Line Interface"
.P
The following flags control hx mode in hashpipe:
.TS
box;
l l.
\fBFlag\fP	\fBMeaning\fP
_
\-X \fIexpr\fP	Evaluate hx expression
\-F \fIfile\fP	Run hx script file
\-p \fIpass\fP	Single password (otherwise stdin)
\-s \fIsalt\fP	Salt value (shared with statfile flag)
\-S \fIsalt2\fP	Second salt value
\-P \fIpepper\fP	Pepper value
\-u \fIuser\fP	User/userid value
.TE
.TB "hashpipe hx Mode Flags"
.P
The
.B \-X
and
.B \-F
flags are mutually exclusive.
When either is present, hashpipe enters hx mode and
does not perform its normal hash verification pipeline.
.P
The
.B \-s
flag serves double duty: in normal hashpipe mode it
specifies the statistics file; in hx mode it provides
the salt value.
This dual use is intentional to minimize the number of
new flags required.
.H 2 "User-Defined Hash Types"
.P
An hx expression may be registered as a user-defined
hash type for use in mdxfind and hashpipe, with no C code
and no recompile.
The expression is placed in a
.B userdef.txt
stanza file and selected in mdxfind with
.B "\-m u<id>" ,
producing matches labelled
.B "USER_<name>xNN" .
hashpipe loads the same file and identifies
.B USER_*
matches in pipe mode \(em a candidate line such as
.DS
.ft CW
echo <digest>:<password> | hashpipe
.ft R
.DE
reports the matching user type alongside any matching
built-in type.
The full stanza format, the
.B "\-m u<id>"
selector, the load-time advisories, and the v1 CPU and
unsalted scope are documented in the mdxfind manual under
"User-Defined Hash Types".
.H 1 "Worked Examples"
.PF "'Worked Examples' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
The following examples demonstrate hx expressions for
common hash schemes, with explanations of each step.
All examples can be executed with
.B "hashpipe \-X"
and produce verifiably correct output.
.H 2 "MD5 (e1)"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'md5(pass)' -p password
5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99
.ft R
.DE
.P
The simplest case.
.B md5()
computes the MD5 digest of the password and returns
it as 32 lowercase hex characters.
.H 2 "SHA-1 of MD5 with Salt (e587)"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)' -p password -s test
d9365e268de8b33a989c390ebaf3f12246b281da
.ft R
.DE
.P
Evaluation proceeds inside-out:
.B md5(pass)
produces the 32-character hex string
.ft CW
5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99\fR,
.ft R
which is concatenated with
.B salt
(\(lqtest\(rq) to form a 36-character string,
which is then hashed with SHA-1.
.H 2 "Triple MD5 (MD5x03)"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'md5^3(pass)' -p password
5a22e6c339c96c9c0513a46e44c39683
.ft R
.DE
.P
Equivalent to
.ft CW
md5(md5(md5(pass)))\fR.
.ft R
Each round hashes the 32-character hex output of the
previous round.
.H 2 "Truncated SHA-512 (Partial Match)"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'cut(sha512(pass), 0, 32)' -p password
b109f3bbbc244eb82441917ed06d618b
.ft R
.DE
.P
The full SHA-512 hex output is 128 characters.
.B cut()
extracts the first 32, producing the same string that
mdxfind would match against a 32-character hex hash file
when testing SHA-512 candidates.
.H 2 "PHPBB3 / phpass"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'h = md5_bin(salt . pass); \\
    for i = 1 to 2047 { h = md5_bin(h . pass) }; \\
    "$H$9" . salt . phpass_encode(h)' \\
    -p password -s saltsalt
$H$9saltsaltWxax0v4yfV8oT17RChGlx1
.ft R
.DE
.P
This is the complete PHPBB3 computation in a single
expression.
The
.B $H$9
prefix indicates the hash variant and iteration count
(2\fISU\fP11\fR = 2048 iterations).
The salt is concatenated literally.
The final hash value is encoded with
.B phpass_encode()
using the itoa64 alphabet, not hex.
.P
Key observations:
.BL
.LI
.B md5_bin()
is used throughout because phpass chains raw binary
digests, not hex strings.
.LI
The initial hash includes the salt:
.ft CW
md5_bin(salt . pass)\fR.
.ft R
.LI
Each subsequent round concatenates the original password
to the previous binary result:
.ft CW
md5_bin(h . pass)\fR.
.ft R
This is the pattern that requires a
.B for
loop rather than the
.B ^
operator.
.LI
The final
.ft CW
phpass_encode(h)
.ft R
produces 22 characters of itoa64-encoded output from the
16-byte MD5 digest.
.LE
.H 2 "MySQL Native Password"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'upper(sha1(sha1_bin(pass)))' -p password
.ft R
.DE
.P
MySQL's native password format is SHA-1 of the raw SHA-1
digest, with uppercase hex output.
The
.B _bin
suffix on the inner SHA-1 is critical: the outer SHA-1
receives 20 raw bytes, not the 40-character hex string.
.H 2 "HMAC-SHA1"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'hmac_sha1(pass, salt)' -p key -s message
2088df74d5f2146b48146caf4965377e9d0be3a4
.ft R
.DE
.P
The first argument is the HMAC key, the second is the
message.
The result is the 40-character hex encoding of the
20-byte HMAC-SHA1 output.
.H 2 "Base64-Encoded MD5"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'base64(md5_bin(pass))' -p password
X03MO1qnZdYdgyfeuILPmQ==
.ft R
.DE
.P
This produces the same output as
.ft CW
echo -n password | openssl md5 -binary | openssl base64\fR.
.ft R
The
.B _bin
suffix is essential: base64 encodes the raw 16-byte
digest, not the 32-character hex string.
.H 2 "Compound with String Literal"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'md5("prefix" . pass . "$")' -p password
.ft R
.DE
.P
String literals can appear anywhere in a concatenation.
This computes MD5 of the string
\(lqprefixpassword$\(rq.
.H 2 "Last N Characters of Password"
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'md5(cut(pass, \-6))' -p helloworld
.ft R
.DE
.P
.B cut()
with a negative offset extracts from the end.
This computes
.ft CW
md5("oworld")\fR.
.ft R
.H 1 "Debugging"
.PF "'Debugging' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
hx provides two diagnostic flags that expose the internal
representation of a compiled program.
.H 2 "AST Dump (\-d)"
.P
The
.B \-d
flag (standalone hx only) prints the abstract syntax tree
to standard error:
.DS
.ft CW
$ hx -d 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)'
BLOCK (1 stmts)
  CALL sha1 (nargs=1)
    CONCAT
      CALL md5 (nargs=1)
        VAR pass
      VAR salt
.ft R
.DE
.P
The tree shows the nesting of function calls and the
concatenation structure, matching the evaluation order.
.H 2 "Bytecode Dump (\-b)"
.P
The
.B \-b
flag prints the compiled bytecode:
.DS
.ft CW
$ hx -b 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)'
--- hx bytecode: 6 instructions, 4 vars, 0 strings ---
  var[0] = pass
  var[1] = salt
  var[2] = salt2
  var[3] = pepper
    0  PUSH_VAR   0 (pass)
    1  CALL       md5 nargs=1
    2  PUSH_VAR   1 (salt)
    3  CONCAT
    4  CALL       sha1 nargs=1
    5  HALT
.ft R
.DE
.P
The bytecode shows the flat instruction sequence that
the VM executes.
Arguments are pushed left to right, function calls consume
their arguments and push the result.
The variable slot assignments, string constant table, and
instruction addresses are all visible.
.H 1 "The Algorithm Catalog"
.PF "'The Algorithm Catalog' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
Every algorithm that mdxfind recognises is described by
exactly one entry in the hx algorithm catalog.
The catalog is presented as Appendix A
.RB ( hx (8))
and is published alongside this specification as
.IR https://www.mdxfind.com/hx.pdf .
Each entry consists of an
.B eN
type number, a short symbolic name, and the
.I hx expression
that defines the computation.
.P
The hx expression
.I is
the definition of the algorithm.
The symbolic name is a mnemonic; the type number is a
stable identifier used at the command line and in
.B mdxfind
output; the hx expression is the authoritative,
machine-readable specification of what the algorithm
computes.
When a researcher asks \(lqwhat does
.B e587
mean?\(rq the answer is the hx expression
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
.ft R
\(em not a paragraph of English prose.
.H 2 "Catalog Stewardship"
.P
A single shared catalog binds the mdxfind family of tools
together.
.B mdxfind
uses the catalog to assign type numbers and report matches.
.B hashpipe
uses the catalog to verify hashes and to evaluate ad-hoc
expressions via the
.B \-X
flag.
.B mdsplit
uses the catalog to associate matched output with the
correct symbolic name.
A correction applied to a catalog entry is therefore a
correction that propagates uniformly across every tool that
consumes the catalog.
.P
The catalog is maintained as a plain-text troff document
.RB ( hx (8))
in the same source tree as this specification.
When a discrepancy is identified between a catalog entry
and the implementation in
.BR mdxfind ,
the entry is corrected in
.BR hx (8)
and republished.
The expression text in the catalog is the canonical
reference; the C implementation in
.B mdxfind
exists to compute the same result at higher throughput.
.H 2 "From Catalog to Bytecode"
.P
The catalog is compiled to a bytecode representation
at build time by the
.B hx8_to_c
tool, which parses
.BR hx (8),
emits a C source file
.RB ( codegen/hx_specs_data.c )
that contains the bytecode and metadata for every entry,
and links it into both
.B mdxfind
and
.BR hashpipe .
.P
The bytecode is the same instruction set described in
Section 2.6: a flat array of
.BR PUSH_VAR ,
.BR PUSH_STR ,
.BR CALL ,
.BR CONCAT ,
and the small number of control-flow opcodes that the
language requires.
A single VM implementation interprets the bytecode for
every entry in the catalog.
There is no per-algorithm C code in the hx runtime;
adding a new algorithm to the catalog adds a new bytecode
program, not a new function.
.P
The bytecode representation supports tooling beyond
straight execution.
A bytecode walker can identify duplicates and near-duplicates
across the catalog, compute structural fingerprints for
algorithm families, and serve as the input to alternative
backends.
.H 1 "Cross-Architecture Execution"
.PF "'Cross-Architecture Execution' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
The hx VM is platform-independent.
The bytecode produced from a catalog entry runs identically
on any CPU architecture that
.B mdxfind
or
.B hashpipe
supports: x86-64, ARM64, POWER, SPARC.
Bit-for-bit reproducibility is a property of the language,
not of any particular host.
.P
For algorithms eligible for GPU acceleration, the same
bytecode also drives the GPU execution path.
A backend-specific code emitter consumes the bytecode and
produces kernel source for the target accelerator:
OpenCL source for NVIDIA, AMD, and Intel devices, and
Metal source for Apple Silicon GPUs.
The emitted kernel computes the same expression that the
CPU VM interprets; the two outputs are byte-exact for the
same inputs.
.P
The user selects the algorithm by its
.B eN
number and the device by the
.B \-G
flag; the backend choice is transparent.
The expression
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
.ft R
\(em catalog entry
.B e587
\(em produces the same 40-character hex string whether the
work runs on an x86-64 thread on a desktop, an ARM64 thread
on Apple Silicon, an OpenCL workgroup on an NVIDIA GTX 1080,
or a Metal threadgroup on an Apple M2 Max.
.P
This architecture has a specific consequence for catalog
authors: an algorithm needs only one definition.
The hx expression in
.BR hx (8)
is consumed by the CPU VM, by the OpenCL emitter, and by
the Metal emitter.
A correction to the expression takes effect on every
backend on the next build.
.H 1 "Tooling"
.PF "'Tooling' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
hx is supported by a small collection of host-side tools.
.H 2 "hx8_to_c"
.P
.B hx8_to_c
is the catalog compiler.
It parses
.BR hx (8),
validates every expression against the grammar of
Section 2.2 and the function registry of Section 3,
and emits a C source file containing the bytecode,
the variable slot map, the string constant table, and
the metadata for each entry.
The output is the single source of truth that
.B mdxfind
and
.B hashpipe
link against; both tools observe the same catalog and the
same compiled bytecode.
.P
A typical invocation:
.DS
.ft CW
hx8_to_c hx.8 > codegen/hx_specs_data.c
.ft R
.DE
.P
Diagnostics are written to standard error.
If any entry fails to compile, the tool reports the
.B eN
number, the source line in
.BR hx (8),
and a description of the error; the offending entry must
be corrected before the build can proceed.
.H 2 "hashpipe \-X"
.P
The
.B \-X
flag to
.B hashpipe
accepts an hx expression on the command line, compiles it
in process, and evaluates it for the supplied inputs.
This is the recommended way to construct, debug, and
verify hx expressions:
.DS
.ft CW
hashpipe -X 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)' -p password -s test
.ft R
.DE
.P
.B \-F
takes the same role for multi-statement programs read
from a file.
Both flags accept the standard hashpipe input modes:
a single password via
.BR \-p ,
a stream of passwords on standard input, or a wordlist
file.
.H 2 "hashpipe catalog smoke tests"
.P
.B hashpipe
treats the catalog as a regression target.
For every entry that has known test vectors, hashpipe can
verify that the compiled bytecode produces the expected
output for the documented inputs.
This is the mechanism by which catalog drift is detected
and corrected.
.H 2 "Standalone hx interpreter"
.P
For language development and pedagogy, a standalone
.B hx
interpreter is provided.
It reads a program from a file or the command line,
compiles it, and runs it against inputs supplied on the
command line.
The
.B \-d
and
.B \-b
flags described in Section 7 dump the AST and the
compiled bytecode respectively, exposing the internal
representation for inspection.
.PF "'Summary' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.H 1 "Summary"
.P
hx is a hash expression language designed for precision,
clarity, and longevity.
Its notation is unambiguous: the expression
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
.ft R
means exactly one thing, and evaluating it produces
exactly one result, which matches the output of
.ft CW
mdxfind \-m e587
.R
on every supported CPU and GPU.
.P
The language's deliberately limited scope \(em hash
functions, concatenation, encoding, iteration, and
simple control flow \(em is a strength, not a limitation.
By constraining the domain, hx ensures that every
expression is a complete, verifiable specification of
a hash computation, suitable for documentation, for
test vector generation, and for independent verification
of results produced by high-performance tools.
.P
The algorithm catalog
.RB ( hx (8),
published as
.IR https://www.mdxfind.com/hx.pdf )
is the canonical reference for every type that
.B mdxfind
recognises.
A catalog entry is its hx expression; the
.B eN
type number and the symbolic name are convenient
identifiers, but the expression is the definition.
The same expression drives the CPU VM in
.B hashpipe ,
the high-throughput implementations in
.BR mdxfind ,
and the GPU kernels emitted for OpenCL and Metal
backends.
.P
Implementation source is published at
.I https://github.com/Cynosureprime/mdxfind
and
.IR https://github.com/Cynosureprime/hashpipe ,
and the troff source of this specification at
.IR https://github.com/Cynosureprime/hx .
As the standard description format for hash computations,
hx bridges the gap between the compressed type names
used by mdxfind internally and the human-readable
descriptions needed by researchers, documentation, and
the broader security community.
